The trainable unit is a rank-$8$ low-rank adapter with its down-projection frozen at a shared public initialisation, together with a classifier head. We use no labelled public data anywhere.
%%
One difference bounds the federation's own contribution independently of the
backbone, because we measure both of its terms on the same backbone. At $\Nc=5$
and $\alpha=0.1$ we measure $\Aloc=0.384$ against $\Asel=0.949$. That difference is
what the protocol supplies.
%%
The cryptographic cost of the protocol is dominated by
one-time setup, and the recurring per-query traffic is a few megabytes.
We do not simulate the cryptography. We implement the protocol in real
multiparty CKKS on Lattigo~\cite{lattigo,mouchet2021multiparty}. All figures use ring degree $2^{14}$ for the shallow operations
and a deeper chain at ring degree $2^{15}$ for the circuits that need it. Timings
are single-run wall clock on a commodity CPU. Communication figures are exact.
%%
\paragraph{Necessity of the partition} The client that sends an inference query must compute
the features of that query itself, by \textbf{C7}. If the shared trained
quantity were an adapter placed inside the backbone, the features would depend on
it, and computing them would require evaluating the backbone with encrypted
weights. The client would then have to evaluate every nonlinearity of the network
homomorphically, which is the cost this design exists to avoid. The trained
quantity that is shared must therefore attach after the last nonlinearity, and in
a transformer, the only such point is the final linear map. That is the head.
%%
\paragraph{Encryption of the query} The client could send the
features in plaintext, which would make the head application cheaper. We encrypt
them because features are invertible. A party holding $\varphi_j(x)$ and the public
backbone can reconstruct an approximation of $x$. Sending features in plaintext
would therefore hand the server the client's input, which is the thing the
protocol exists to protect.
%%
A single-key scheme would require one party to hold the secret key and therefore
to be trusted with decryption. We use the multiparty variant of
CKKS~\cite{mouchet2021multiparty}, where the secret key is shared among the
clients through a distributed key-generation protocol that produces a collective
public key without ever assembling the secret in one place. Any party, the server
included, may compute on ciphertexts under the collective public key, but
decryption requires the cooperation of a quorum of clients. We use two further protocols of the same family
below. The first is a key switch to a designated public key, which re-encrypts
a result so that one chosen party can read it. The second is a collective refresh,
which restores a depleted level budget and plays the role bootstrapping plays in
the single-key setting.
%%
Secure aggregation lets the server learn only the sum of the clients' updates,
using masks that cancel in the aggregate~\cite{bonawitz2017practical}. It
protects one round's sum inside an iterative protocol, and
Kerkouche et al. show that the revealed per-round sums themselves leak across
rounds~\cite{kerkouche2023client}. POSEIDON runs encrypted
stochastic gradient descent under multiparty CKKS, with activations replaced by
polynomial approximations~\cite{sav2021poseidon}. The approach is iterative and encrypted nonlinearity costs
a deep multiplicative circuit with repeated bootstrapping.
That cost scales with the network being trained, so adapting a pretrained
transformer by this route is out of reach.
%%
defines the ideal functionality $\Fhe$ against which we
measure the protocol. The server is an entity outside $\Fhe$, so it may be corrupt. The form of the functionality follows the
secure aggregation functionality of ELSA~\cite{rathee2023elsa}, and placing the
aggregator outside it follows FULLSA~\cite{karakoc2024fullsa}.
%%
We evaluate on four text classification tasks of increasing label-space size,
AG-News with $4$ classes, TREC with $6$, DBpedia with $14$ and Banking77 with
$77$, using a frozen RoBERTa-base encoder~\cite{liu2019roberta}, and on CIFAR-100
with a frozen ViT-B/16~\cite{dosovitskiy2021vit}. Each text task draws at most
$20{,}000$ training and $5{,}000$ test examples under the seed, and each vision
task at most $10{,}000$ and $2{,}000$. Only TREC and Banking77 fall below these
caps, and we use those whole. Data are partitioned across clients by a Dirichlet distribution over
labels with concentration $\alpha$, the standard label-skew
partition~\cite{hsu2019measuring,li2022niidbench}, which induces label-distribution
skew in the usual taxonomy~\cite{kairouz2021advances} and holds the feature
distribution fixed across clients. A smaller $\alpha$ leaves each client a few
dominant classes and almost none of the rest. Unless stated otherwise there are $\Nc=10$
clients, $\alpha=0.1$, the local trajectory is $200$ steps, and every number is the
mean over three seeds. Each client reserves at least one held-out example from
every class it holds, as   requires, and tops the held-out set
up to one tenth of its shard at random.
%%
The more informative reading concerns which arrangement the estimator selects. The estimator
chooses the shared head at $\alpha\in\{0.05,0.1\}$ and the personal adapter at
$\alpha\in\{0.3,1.0\}$, on every seed in each case. The crossover is therefore not
a property of the label space alone, as   might suggest, but of
coverage. The personal adapter is preferable once each client sees enough of the
label space for its own representation to transfer, and the shared head is
preferable when it does not. The estimator locates that crossover from encrypted
local evidence, with no test set and no fitted threshold.
%%
The failure is structural. The shared arrangement is one
model, and the union of the clients' data is the whole training set, so pooled
held-out measurements estimate its accuracy on the global distribution to within
$0.02$ on average. The personal arrangement is $\Nc$ different models, and client $j$'s
held-out data measures only client $j$'s own model, on client $j$'s own
distribution, which is the distribution that model was fitted to. Its measured
accuracy exceeds its true global accuracy by $0.28$ on average. A client's
held-out set also contains only the classes that client holds, so the very
quantity that decides the comparison, accuracy on classes the client has never
seen, the client cannot measure locally at all. The procedure compares an accurate number
against an optimistic one, so it is not close to correct and it does not become
correct with more held-out data.
%%
\paragraph{Encryption of the query} The client could send the
features in plaintext, which would make the head application cheaper. We encrypt
them because features are invertible. A party holding $\varphi_j(x)$ and the public
backbone can reconstruct an approximation of $x$. Sending features in plaintext
would therefore hand the server the client's input, which is the thing the
protocol exists to protect. The price is that the server applies the head ciphertext by
ciphertext rather than ciphertext by plaintext, and   reports
what that costs.
%%
Secure aggregation lets the server learn only the sum of the clients' updates,
using masks that cancel in the aggregate~\cite{bonawitz2017practical}. It is
lossless, but it protects one round's sum inside an iterative protocol, and
Kerkouche et al. and So et al. show that the revealed per-round sums themselves leak across
rounds~\cite{so2023securing,kerkouche2023client}. Homomorphic encryption goes
further by computing on contributions that stay encrypted. POSEIDON runs encrypted
stochastic gradient descent under multiparty CKKS, with activations replaced by
polynomial approximations so that the server can evaluate them
homomorphically~\cite{sav2021poseidon}. The guarantee is strong and the model
loses no accuracy, but the approach is iterative and encrypted nonlinearity costs
a deep multiplicative circuit with repeated bootstrapping. Several works measure
how delicate those polynomial activations
are~\cite{garimella2021sisyphus,baruch2022methodology,agamennone2025polynomial,alhossain2025training,ibarrondo2021fhebn}.
That cost scales with the network being trained, so adapting a pretrained
transformer by this route is out of reach.
%%
# 2026-08-27, the client-to-client privacy subsection. Ten new paragraphs, all
# of them report-only, all of them inside \tronly, so the submission is
# unchanged and gate 3 still reports 0 rewritten. They exist because Theorem 2
# bounds a coalition by negl + delta and never says what delta is, which lets a
# reviewer read the theorem as vacuous. The subsection names delta and bounds
# it. The heading is new because the subsection is new. Appended at the end of
# Section IV so no shared section label changes number, which check_split.py
# requires.
\subsection{What a Fellow Client Learns}
\label{sec:c2c}
%%
# 2026-08-27, as above. States what the subsection settles.
\Cref{thm:malicious} bounds a deviating coalition by
$\negl(\lambda)+\delta(Q_{\mathrm{tot}})$ and leaves $\delta$ unnamed. We name it
here. A coalition of clients is the one adversary the cryptography does not
silence, because its members hold key shares, run the backbone themselves, and
receive answers by design.
%%
# 2026-08-27, as above. Enumerates the fellow client's view in its own terms.
# Every item restates what Functionality 1's leakage already allows, and the
# two negatives restate what Section III-B and Section III-A already build.
A client outside the coalition contributes four things to what the
coalition sees. It contributes the public sample count $\nj$, which the protocol
uses as a public scalar. It contributes the ciphertexts it uploads, which are
computationally independent of what they encrypt. It contributes its share of the
announced index $a^{\star}$ and of the phase signals. It contributes its part of
every answer the coalition is given. It contributes no per-class total, because
\cref{sec:split} inverts those under encryption and never decrypts them, and it
contributes no adapter, because no adapter aggregate exists.
%%
# 2026-08-27, as above. Concedes the objection that the allowance is not a
# membership bound, and says what is bounded instead. The citation is
# choquettechoo2021labelonly, which was already in refs.bib and cited nowhere.
The allowance alone does not bound what the answers reveal. Membership
inference from predicted labels is an established attack on a classifier that
answers queries~\cite{choquettechoo2021labelonly}, and its cost is not a function
the allowance controls. We therefore bound the channel at the head rather than at
the budget.
%%
# 2026-08-27, as above. The statement. It is a reduction, so it needs no
# measurement and it holds at every budget.
\begin{proposition}[The query channel is no stronger than the head]
\label{prop:c2c}
Let $\mathcal{A}$ corrupt at most $\tc-1$ clients in \cref{def:contentgame}, with
the server honest, and let it ask at most $Q_{\mathrm{tot}}=(\tc-1)Q$ queries.
Then there is an adversary $\mathcal{B}$ that receives $\thstar$ in plaintext
together with $\Leak$, asks no query, and satisfies
\[
  \mathsf{Adv}(\mathcal{A})\;\le\;\mathsf{Adv}(\mathcal{B})+\negl(\lambda).
\]
\end{proposition}
%%
# 2026-08-27, as above. Proof, first of three paragraphs. It reuses the view
# decomposition of Theorem 2's own proof rather than establishing a new one.
\begin{proof}
The proof of \cref{thm:malicious} splits the view of $\mathcal{A}$ into the
key-generation transcript, the honest clients' ciphertexts, the values the honest
server computes, and the answers to its own queries. The first three are
independent of $b$ up to $\negl(\lambda)$, and $\mathcal{B}$ produces them from
$\Leak$ and from encryptions of zero exactly as the simulator of
\cref{thm:semihonest} does.
%%
# 2026-08-27, as above. Proof, second paragraph. The step that does the work is
# that the querying client is corrupt, so the adversary already holds the
# adapter that defines the feature map.
Each answer is $\arg\max_c(\thstar\,\varphi_j(x_i))_c$ for a query $x_i$ that
$\mathcal{A}$ asks at one of its own clients $j$. The map $\varphi_j$ composes the
public backbone with the adapter of client $j$. That client is corrupt, so
$\mathcal{B}$ holds its adapter, and $\mathcal{B}$ holds $\thstar$ by assumption.
It computes every answer itself.
%%
# 2026-08-27, as above. Proof, third paragraph.
$\mathcal{B}$ therefore runs $\mathcal{A}$, answers each query as the server would,
and returns whatever $\mathcal{A}$ returns. The simulation is exact outside the
negligible event on which the first three parts of the view differ.
\end{proof}
%%
# 2026-08-27, as above. The corollary, which is the sentence the report needs.
# It makes Theorem 2 non-vacuous without any measurement, and it says what the
# allowance does control, which is cost.
Write $\delta_{\mathrm{wb}}$ for the advantage of the best
polynomial-time adversary that holds $\thstar$. \Cref{prop:c2c} gives
$\delta(Q_{\mathrm{tot}})\le\delta_{\mathrm{wb}}$ at every budget. What a
coalition learns about an honest client is capped by what the shared head itself
reveals, and that cap does not depend on the allowance. The allowance prices the
cost of approaching the cap, and \cref{sec:exp-leak} measures that cost.
%%
# 2026-08-27, as above. What the deployment does with it. This replaces nothing
# and it is what makes the threshold discussion in Section V-G precise, since
# the threshold moves the cost and not the cap.
Two consequences follow for a deployment. Lowering the threshold $\tc$
raises $Q_{\mathrm{tot}}$ and moves a coalition along the curve toward the cap,
and it does not move the cap. Raising the allowance does the same. Neither choice
changes what is ultimately learnable, so both are decisions about cost.
%%
# 2026-08-27, the noise-on-labels measurement. Report-only. It exists because
# the scope paragraph claimed the mechanism was not evaluated while
# results/extraction_defence/results.csv evaluates it over three tasks, six
# budgets and three seeds. That claim is now deleted and this reports the
# measurement instead. It is also the answer to the reviewer who asks why
# differential privacy is not applied to the interface.
\paragraph{Noise on the returned label} The query allowance is one way to
price extraction. Perturbing the answer is the other, and it is what a reader who
knows differential privacy will ask for first. We measure it. The server flips the
returned label under randomised response at budget $\varepsilon$, which is the
label-level mechanism~\cite{mcsherry2007mechanism}, and we record both what the
copy reaches and what the served model still scores.
\Cref{tab:defence} reports the result.
%%
# 2026-08-27, the caption of the new table. Every figure in it is the mean over
# three seeds from results/extraction_defence/results.csv, recomputed
# 2026-08-27. The majority shares are from results/extraction_budget.
Randomised response on the returned label. Copy fidelity after
$10^5$ label queries, against the accuracy the served model retains, at each
budget $\varepsilon$. Mean over three seeds. The majority share a constant
predictor achieves is $0.488$ on AG-News, $0.250$ on DBpedia and $0.083$ on
Banking77.
%%
# 2026-08-27, what the table shows. Each clause is checked against the record
# and stated per task, because no single claim holds across all three. The
# earlier note in the plan quoted 0.310, 0.111 and 0.014 at eps=1, which mixes
# eps=1 with eps=0.5 and does not match the file. These are the file's numbers.
The mechanism does not separate the two quantities. At $\varepsilon=8$ it
moves neither, costing AG-News $0.001$ of accuracy and the copy $0.003$ of
fidelity. Below that both fall together. On AG-News the budget that first costs
the copy a fifth of its fidelity is $\varepsilon=1$, where the copy still reaches
$0.774$ and the served model has fallen to $0.372$, under the $0.488$ share of its
most common class. On DBpedia accuracy falls by a larger fraction than fidelity at
every budget below $\varepsilon=8$. On Banking77 the copy does degrade faster than
the task below $\varepsilon=4$, and only after accuracy has reached $0.028$
against a majority share of $0.083$, so the model the noise protects is already
worse than a constant predictor.
%%
# 2026-08-27, why the allowance is the right control. This is the argument the
# measurement was run to settle, and without it the table is data with no
# conclusion.
This is the argument for metering queries rather than perturbing answers.
Noise on the label is charged to every honest query and to every extraction query
at the same rate, and the extraction campaign spends $10^5$ queries where an
honest client spends one. The allowance charges the two differently, which is the
property the mechanism lacks. We therefore state the operational bound as a bound
on queries, and we do not add noise to the answers.
%%
# 2026-08-27, communication against the client count. Report-only. TNSE is a
# networks venue and neither document said how the protocol scales in the size
# of the federation, although results/fhe_serve/comm_grid.json answers it.
# Verified 2026-08-27 across n_parties 5, 10 and 20, log_n 14, 15 and 16, and
# both chains: no per-party field varies with the client count.
\paragraph{How this scales in the federation} Every per-party quantity in
\cref{tab:comm} is independent of the client count. We measured the grid at
$\Nc\in\{5,10,20\}$, at ring degrees $2^{14}$, $2^{15}$ and $2^{16}$, and on both
the aggregation and the serving chain, and the collective public key share, the
relinearization key share, the rotation key share, the ciphertext sizes and the
key-switching share are the same at every client count. This is what the structure
of the scheme predicts, since a party's share is a function of the ring and the
modulus chain and not of how many parties there are, and the measurement confirms
it rather than discovering it.
%%
# 2026-08-27, what that scaling costs a large federation. The two totals are
# arithmetic on the 344 MiB per-client setup figure already in the section.
Two consequences set the cost of a large federation. What a client sends
does not grow as clients are added, so setup stays at $344$\,MiB for each of them
and the federation's total key-generation traffic grows linearly in $\Nc$, which is
$3.4$\,GiB at $\Nc=10$ and $17$\,GiB at $\Nc=50$. A query costs the querier a fixed
$8.5$\,MiB and the quorum one key-switching share each, so per-query traffic grows
linearly in $\tc$ and not in $\Nc$. A deployment that lowers the threshold for
availability therefore also lowers the traffic on every query, and
\cref{sec:c2c} states what it costs in privacy, which is a shorter distance to the
same cap rather than a higher cap.
%%
# 2026-08-27, the liveness limitation. Section II says a quorum means serving
# does not need every client online, and the implementation runs t=N, where it
# does. Both statements are true and the report did not put them next to each
# other. A networks venue requires this limitation to be stated.
The liveness cost of that choice should be stated plainly. The scheme
admits any $\tc$ with $2\le\tc\le\Nc$, and at a general threshold a query needs
$\tc$ clients rather than all of them, which is what makes serving survive an
offline participant. Our implementation sets $\tc=\Nc$, so in the configuration we
measured a single unavailable client blocks every query until it returns. A
deployment that needs serving to continue through churn sets $\tc<\Nc$ and accepts
coalitions of that size.
%%
# 2026-08-27, the threshold trade-off restated against the new bound. Before
# Proposition 2 the report could only say that lowering the threshold tightens
# the allowance. It can now say the ceiling does not move, which is the more
# useful statement and the one a deployment needs.
\Cref{sec:c2c} makes the price of that choice smaller than it appears.
Lowering $\tc$ raises the coalition's total allowance $(\tc-1)Q$, so a coalition
reaches a given fidelity sooner. It does not raise the ceiling
$\delta_{\mathrm{wb}}$ on what a coalition can learn about an honest client, since
that ceiling is a property of the shared head. The threshold therefore trades
availability against the cost of an attack rather than against what the attack can
ultimately achieve, and a deployment can buy liveness with a proportionate
reduction in $Q$.
%%
# 2026-08-27, which mechanism produced the reported latency. Report-only, added
# on Halil's instruction to correct the timing. The reported 31.5 s and 113.2 s
# come from results/fhe_serve/argmax_tournament.csv, which the case README
# records as collective-refresh-backed. The paragraph above specifies the other
# mechanism, server-side restoration under collectively generated bootstrapping
# keys, and that variant has never been timed. Without this the document reports
# a latency for one design and a per-query traffic figure for the other. Every
# number is from that CSV: 9 and 34 refreshes, 12273.3 ms and 46319.7 ms in
# refresh, 18970.1 ms and 66649.9 ms in local evaluation. The ring degrees are
# from the same CSV and from btp_keys.json.
\paragraph{Which mechanism produced the reported latency} The figures
above were measured with collective-refresh-backed level restoration, and not
with the bootstrapping keys the protocol specifies. The tournament spends nine
collective refreshes at four classes and thirty-four at a hundred, and those
refreshes account for $12.3$\,s of the $31.5$\,s and for $46.3$\,s of the
$113.2$\,s. The remaining $19.0$\,s and $66.6$\,s are local evaluation, which
both mechanisms pay alike. Under the specified design the server restores levels
on its own under the collectively generated keys, which replaces the refresh
component by a server-side bootstrap that we have not timed. The ring degree
differs as well, since we take the timings at $2^{15}$ and generate the
bootstrapping keys at $2^{16}$. We therefore report these figures as the latency
of the collective-refresh variant, and we do not claim them for the variant the
protocol specifies.
